<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bujang Valley</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bujang Valley is an archaeological region near Merbok in Kedah, north-western Malaysia, between Gunung Jerai and the Muda River. It contains the remains of more than fifty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist brick and laterite shrines, called candi, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a> site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sungai-batu">Sungai Batu</a>, dating from the early centuries CE to about the 14th century. The sites show Old Kedah as an early port linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Malay world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bujang-valley.jpg" alt="Bujang Valley" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gryffindor · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muzium_Negara_KL19b.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Bujang Valley, Lembah Bujang in Malay, lies between the 1,217-metre Mount Jerai in the north and the Muda River in the south, and is the richest archaeological area in Malaysia. The complex covers about 224 square kilometres, and the discovery of the iron-smelting sites at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sungai-batu">Sungai Batu</a> has enlarged the known area of settlement to about 1,000 square kilometres. Its best-preserved candi stands at Pengkalan Bujang, Merbok.</p><p>The name is usually traced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> bhujangga. One reading takes it to mean scholar, giving Scholars' Valley; researchers of Universiti Sains Malaysia and other institutions take it to mean serpent, referring to the winding course of the Muda and Merbok rivers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ptolemy wrote of the Golden Chersonese, a sign that trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> reached the region from the 1st century CE. Placed at the north-western entrance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> and facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, the valley was visited by Chinese and south Indian traders, as trade ceramics, sculptures, inscriptions and monuments of the 5th to 14th centuries show. It was ruled in turn by sea powers including Funan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> before its trade declined.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sungai-batu">Sungai Batu</a>, excavations have found jetty remains, iron-smelting sites and a clay brick monument dated to 110 CE, the oldest man-made structure recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. An inscribed stone bar bears the Buddhist ye-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> hetu formula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallava-script">Pallava script</a>. Research before the 1970s was led by H. G. Quaritch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, Dorothy Wales and Alastair Lamb; later Malaysian archaeologists, above all Nik Hassan Shuhaimi Nik Abdul Rahman, continued it, and after 2008 a team under Mohd Mokhtar Saidin found dozens of new sites said to date from the 2nd century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Finds from the valley, among them inscribed stone caskets, metal tools, ceramics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> images, are shown in the Bujang Valley Archaeological Museum. In 2013 it was reported that Candi No. 11, a temple about 1,200 years old, had been demolished by a land developer. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> endorsed the site in a report of 1987; in 2017 the Malaysian government said more research was needed and left it off the country's tentative list.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bujang Valley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over fifty candi, brick and laterite shrines both Hindu and Buddhist, have been found in the <b>Bujang Valley</b>, along with ironworking sites at Sungai Batu.</blockquote><a href="/state/old-kedah">Old Kedah</a></li></ul></section>
